From: mklab <noreply@example.invalid>
Subject: [PATCH 42/43] a `reg` is decoded with the PARENT's cells, and ide wrote a fixed three

Arch-tested: x86 amd64 ppc

A `reg` property is not self-describing: it is read with the #address-cells and
#size-cells its PARENT declares. drivers/ide.c wrote three cells regardless --
channel, 0, 0 -- and on the PC arches that happened to decode correctly, because
the root says one address cell and the third cell was slop nobody read.

It stops being a happy accident the moment the root declares two (patch 43).
forth/device/pathres.fs's (exact-match) compares the first #address-cells CELLS
of a child's `reg` against whatever the parent's decode-unit returned for the
unit string, so [channel][0] would be read as channel << 32: `/ide@1` would
resolve to nothing and `/ide@100000000` would resolve instead, taking the boot
aliases, the showcase's boot line and every `load` off the CD with it.

Derive the layout from the parent. Measured before and after with the root still
at ONE address cell, so the change is separable from the one that needed it:
/ide@0..3 keep their names and `reg` goes from three cells to two (one address,
one size), because the third was never anything but padding.

NOT the parent's problem to guess at. ide_node_parent() reads the device-node's
own >dn.parent field rather than my_parent(), which is instance-based and reads
my-self -- whatever the last opened package left behind. This runs during tree
construction, where there is no instance at all.

ppc and sparc64 keep the old three cells byte for byte, under the same
!CONFIG_PPC && !CONFIG_SPARC64 guard the interrupts property above already uses.
They reach this function through drivers/pci.c with a PCI BUS as the parent
(3 address cells), where deriving would move the channel number from the first
cell to the third and rename every node. That is a real defect there too, and
fixing it blind on an arch whose IDE path nothing here boots is how a shared
file breaks two arches to fix one. ppc rebuilt and re-smoked as the control.

--- a/drivers/ide.c
+++ b/drivers/ide.c
@@ -1516,6 +1516,45 @@
 	set_property(aliases, idestr, path, strlen(path) + 1);
 }
 
+#if !defined(CONFIG_PPC) && !defined(CONFIG_SPARC64)
+/* The parent of the node `new-device` just created. NOT my_parent(): that is
+ * instance-based and reads my-self, which is whatever the last opened package
+ * left behind -- this runs during tree construction, where there is no
+ * instance. >dn.parent is the device-node field itself (structures.fs:34). */
+static phandle_t ide_node_parent(phandle_t node)
+{
+	PUSH(node);
+	fword(">dn.parent");
+	return *(phandle_t *)cell2pointer(POP());
+}
+
+static int ide_cells(phandle_t node, const char *name, int dflt)
+{
+	int len;
+	ucell v;
+
+	if (!node)
+		return dflt;
+	v = get_int_property(node, name, &len);
+	/* Absent is not zero: the 1275 defaults are what my-#acells and
+	 * my-#scells fall back to (property.fs:221,234), and disagreeing with
+	 * the tree's own decoder is how a property becomes unreadable. */
+	if (len != (int)sizeof(u32))
+		return dflt;
+	return (int)v;
+}
+
+/* 1275 cells are big-endian, high cell first. */
+static int ide_encode_cells(u32 *prop, int cells, ucell val)
+{
+	int i;
+
+	for (i = cells - 1; i >= 0; i--)
+		prop[cells - 1 - i] = __cpu_to_be32((u32)(val >> (16 * i) >> (16 * i)));
+	return cells;
+}
+#endif
+
 int ob_ide_init(const char *path, uint32_t io_port0, uint32_t ctl_port0,
 		uint32_t io_port1, uint32_t ctl_port1)
 {
@@ -1588,10 +1627,54 @@
 			     (char *)&props, 2*sizeof(props[0]));
 #endif
 
+#if !defined(CONFIG_PPC) && !defined(CONFIG_SPARC64)
+		/* A `reg` IS NOT SELF-DESCRIBING: it is decoded with the PARENT's
+		 * #address-cells / #size-cells, and this wrote a fixed three cells
+		 * regardless of what the parent declares. On the PC arches the parent
+		 * is the root, which says one address cell, so the third cell was
+		 * slop nobody read and the property happened to decode correctly.
+		 *
+		 * It stops being a happy accident the moment the root declares two
+		 * address cells: `(exact-match)` in forth/device/pathres.fs compares
+		 * the first #address-cells CELLS of `reg` against what the parent's
+		 * decode-unit returned for the unit string, so [channel][0] would be
+		 * read as the address (channel << 32) and `/ide@1` would resolve to
+		 * nothing while `/ide@100000000` resolved instead -- taking the boot
+		 * aliases, the showcase's boot line and every `load` off the CD with
+		 * it. Derive the layout from the parent instead of assuming it.
+		 *
+		 * ppc and sparc64 keep the old three cells byte for byte: they reach
+		 * this function through drivers/pci.c with a PCI bus as the parent
+		 * (3 address cells), where deriving would MOVE the channel number from
+		 * the first cell to the third and rename every node. That is a real
+		 * defect there too, and it is not this change's to fix blind on an
+		 * arch whose IDE path nothing here boots. */
+		{
+			phandle_t par = ide_node_parent(dnode);
+			int acells = ide_cells(par, "#address-cells", 2);
+			int scells = ide_cells(par, "#size-cells", 1);
+			int n = 0;
+
+			if (acells < 1 || acells > 2 || scells < 0 || scells > 2) {
+				/* A tree this was not written for. Refuse to write a
+				 * property every consumer would misread. */
+				printk("ide: parent declares #address-cells %d / "
+				       "#size-cells %d -- leaving reg unset\n",
+				       acells, scells);
+			} else {
+				n += ide_encode_cells(props + n, acells,
+						      current_channel);
+				n += ide_encode_cells(props + n, scells, 0);
+				set_property(dnode, "reg", (char *)props,
+					     n * sizeof(props[0]));
+			}
+		}
+#else
 		props[0] = __cpu_to_be32(current_channel);
 		props[1] = __cpu_to_be32(0);
 		props[2] = 0;
 		set_property(dnode, "reg", (char *)&props, 3*sizeof(props[0]));
+#endif
 
 		set_int_property(dnode, "#address-cells", 1);
 		set_int_property(dnode, "#size-cells", 0);
